# Angular 2026 Interview Roadmap - CRITICAL UPDATES SUMMARY

## 🎯 The 5 Game-Changing Updates You MUST Know for 2026

### 1. SIGNAL FORMS (Previously Reactive Forms)

**Status:** Production standard in Angular 21 (2026)
**Impact:** 60% less boilerplate, automatic optimization, type-safe

#### Before (Deprecated Pattern):
```typescript
form = this.fb.group({
  firstName: ['', Validators.required],
  lastName: ['', Validators.required]
});
```

#### After (2026 Standard):
```typescript
user = model<User>({ firstName: '', lastName: '' });
isValid = computed(() => user().firstName.length > 0 && user().lastName.length > 0);
```

**Interview Question:** "Explain why Signal Forms replaced Reactive Forms in 2026."
**Answer:** "Signal Forms are the new standard because they provide type safety at compile time, require zero boilerplate setup compared to FormBuilder/FormGroup, and integrate perfectly with zoneless change detection. They use the `model()` directive for automatic two-way binding with signals, making validation and state management simpler and more performant."

---

### 2. ZONELESS CHANGE DETECTION (Zone.js Deprecated)

**Status:** DEFAULT in Angular 21 (2026)
**Impact:** 60-80% fewer change detection cycles, better performance

#### Zone.js (OLD, 2023 and earlier):
```
User types → Zone.js intercepts → change detection
Network request → Zone.js intercepts → change detection
Timer fires → Zone.js intercepts → change detection
Result: 10+ unnecessary change detection runs
```

#### Zoneless (NEW, 2026):
```
User updates signal → 1 change detection
Network request (no state change) → 0 change detection
Timer fires (no state change) → 0 change detection
Result: 1 precise change detection run
```

**Interview Question:** "How does zoneless change detection improve performance?"
**Answer:** "Zoneless eliminates the Zone.js wrapper that intercepts all async operations. Instead, only signal mutations trigger change detection. This is more precise: we only run change detection when actual state changes occur, not after every async operation. For a typical app with network requests, timers, and user interactions, this reduces change detection cycles by 60-80%."

---

### 3. VITEST REPLACES KARMA (Test Runner)

**Status:** Default in Angular 21 (2026)
**Impact:** 10x faster tests, better debugging, modern tooling

#### Karma (OLD):
- Slow (takes minutes for full suite)
- Complex configuration (karma.conf.js)
- Browser-based testing
- CommonJS first

#### Vitest (NEW):
- Fast (10x faster, runs in Node)
- Simple config (vitest.config.ts)
- ESM-first (modern JavaScript)
- Built-in coverage

**Interview Question:** "Why switch from Karma to Vitest?"
**Answer:** "Karma was designed for older JavaScript patterns and was painfully slow. Vitest uses Vite (modern bundler), is 10x faster, requires minimal configuration, and has better TypeScript integration. TestBed API stays identical, so migration is just changing the test runner. By 2026, Vitest is the only sensible choice for new Angular projects."

---

### 4. @defer BLOCKS (Lazy Loading Components)

**Status:** Stable in Angular 21 (2026)
**Impact:** Smaller initial bundle, better code splitting

#### Old Pattern (*ngIf):
```typescript
<div *ngIf="showChart">
  <app-heavy-chart></app-heavy-chart> <!-- Creates component, just hides it -->
</div>
```
**Problem:** Component always created, just hidden. Uses memory even if not needed.

#### New Pattern (@defer):
```typescript
@defer (on viewport) {
  <app-heavy-chart></app-heavy-chart> <!-- Only created when scrolled into view -->
} @placeholder {
  <p>Loading...</p>
}
```
**Benefit:** Component never created until needed. Doesn't bundle until user navigates there.

**Interview Question:** "When would you use @defer instead of *ngIf?"
**Answer:** "@defer is for non-critical components that can be lazy-loaded. If a heavy chart or report is below the fold and users rarely see it, @defer ensures it's not created until needed. This reduces initial bundle and improves Time-to-Interactive. @defer creates true code splitting, whereas *ngIf just hides the component but still creates it."

---

### 5. MODERN CONTROL FLOW (@if, @for, @switch)

**Status:** Standard in Angular 17+ (consolidated in 21)
**Impact:** Cleaner templates, compile-time checks

#### Old Pattern:
```typescript
<div *ngIf="isLoading">Loading...</div>
<li *ngFor="let item of items">{{ item }}</li>
<div [ngSwitch]="status">
  <div *ngSwitchCase="'done'">Done</div>
</div>
```

#### New Pattern (2026 Standard):
```typescript
@if (isLoading()) { <div>Loading...</div> }

@for (let item of items(); track item.id) { <li>{{ item }}</li> }

@switch (status()) {
  @case ('done') { <div>Done</div> }
  @default { <div>Unknown</div> }
}
```

**Interview Question:** "What's the advantage of @if over *ngIf?"
**Answer:** "@if is more explicit (looks like JavaScript), compiles to more efficient code, and provides compile-time safety. The `track` in @for is mandatory, preventing performance bugs. @switch uses explicit syntax matching. Overall, the new control flow is more readable, safer, and performs better."

